[00003.619686] This pass uses the ABC tool [1] for technology mapping of yosys's internal gate
[00003.619687] library to a target architecture.
[00003.619687] 
[00003.619688]     -exe <command>
[00003.619689]         use the specified command instead of "<yosys-bindir>/yosys-abc" to execute ABC.
[00003.619690]         This can e.g. be used to call a specific version of ABC or a wrapper.
[00003.619690] 
[00003.619690]     -script <file>
[00003.619691]         use the specified ABC script file instead of the default script.
[00003.619691] 
[00003.619692]         if <file> starts with a plus sign (+), then the rest of the filename
[00003.619693]         string is interpreted as the command string to be passed to ABC. The
[00003.619693]         leading plus sign is removed and all commas (,) in the string are
[00003.619694]         replaced with blanks before the string is passed to ABC.
[00003.619694] 
[00003.619695]         if no -script parameter is given, the following scripts are used:
[00003.619695] 
[00003.619696]         for -liberty/-genlib without -constr:
[00003.619698]           strash; &get -n; &fraig -x; &put; scorr; dc2; dretime; strash;
               &get -n; &dch -f; &nf {D}; &put
[00003.619698] 
[00003.619699]         for -liberty/-genlib with -constr:
[00003.619701]           strash; &get -n; &fraig -x; &put; scorr; dc2; dretime; strash;
               &get -n; &dch -f; &nf {D}; &put; buffer; upsize {D};
               dnsize {D}; stime -p
[00003.619701] 
[00003.619702]         for -lut/-luts (only one LUT size):
[00003.619703]           strash; &get -n; &fraig -x; &put; scorr; dc2; dretime; strash;
               dch -f; if; mfs2; lutpack -S 1
[00003.619703] 
[00003.619704]         for -lut/-luts (different LUT sizes):
[00003.619705]           strash; &get -n; &fraig -x; &put; scorr; dc2; dretime; strash;
               dch -f; if; mfs2
[00003.619705] 
[00003.619706]         for -sop:
[00003.619707]           strash; &get -n; &fraig -x; &put; scorr; dc2; dretime; strash;
               dch -f; cover {I} {P}
[00003.619707] 
[00003.619708]         otherwise:
[00003.619709]           strash; &get -n; &fraig -x; &put; scorr; dc2; dretime; strash;
               &get -n; &dch -f; &nf {D}; &put
[00003.619709] 
[00003.619710]     -liberty <file>
[00003.619710]         generate netlists for the specified cell library (using the liberty
[00003.619711]         file format).
[00003.619711] 
[00003.619712]     -dont_use <cell_name>
[00003.619712]         avoid usage of the technology cell <cell_name> when mapping the design.
[00003.619713]         this option can be used multiple times with different cell names and
[00003.619713]         supports simple glob patterns in the cell name.
[00003.619714]         only supported with Liberty cell libraries.
[00003.619714] 
[00003.619715]     -genlib <file>
[00003.619715]         generate netlists for the specified cell library (using the SIS Genlib
[00003.619716]         file format).
[00003.619716] 
[00003.619716]     -constr <file>
[00003.619717]         pass this file with timing constraints to ABC.
[00003.619717]         use with -liberty/-genlib.
[00003.619718] 
[00003.619718]         a constr file contains two lines:
[00003.619719]             set_driving_cell <cell_name>
[00003.619719]             set_load <floating_point_number>
[00003.619720] 
[00003.619720]         the set_driving_cell statement defines which cell type is assumed to
[00003.619721]         drive the primary inputs and the set_load statement sets the load in
[00003.619721]         femtofarads for each primary output.
[00003.619721] 
[00003.619722]     -D <picoseconds>
[00003.619723]         set delay target. the string {D} in the default scripts above is
[00003.619723]         replaced by this option when used, and an empty string otherwise.
[00003.619724]         this also replaces 'dretime' with 'dretime; retime -o {D}' in the
[00003.619729]         default scripts above.
[00003.619730] 
[00003.619730]     -I <num>
[00003.619731]         maximum number of SOP inputs.
[00003.619731]         (replaces {I} in the default scripts above)
[00003.619732] 
[00003.619732]     -P <num>
[00003.619733]         maximum number of SOP products.
[00003.619733]         (replaces {P} in the default scripts above)
[00003.619734] 
[00003.619734]     -lut <width>
[00003.619734]         generate netlist using luts of (max) the specified width.
[00003.619735] 
[00003.619735]     -lut <w1>:<w2>
[00003.619736]         generate netlist using luts of (max) the specified width <w2>. All
[00003.619736]         luts with width <= <w1> have constant cost. for luts larger than <w1>
[00003.619737]         the area cost doubles with each additional input bit. the delay cost
[00003.619738]         is still constant for all lut widths.
[00003.619738] 
[00003.619739]     -luts <cost1>,<cost2>,<cost3>,<sizeN>:<cost4-N>,..
[00003.619739]         generate netlist using luts. Use the specified costs for luts with 1,
[00003.619740]         2, 3, .. inputs.
[00003.619740] 
[00003.619740]     -sop
[00003.619741]         map to sum-of-product cells and inverters
[00003.619741] 
[00003.619742]     -g type1,type2,...
[00003.619743]         Map to the specified list of gate types. Supported gates types are:
[00003.619743]            AND, NAND, OR, NOR, XOR, XNOR, ANDNOT, ORNOT, MUX,
[00003.619744]            NMUX, AOI3, OAI3, AOI4, OAI4.
[00003.619744]         (The NOT gate is always added to this list automatically.)
[00003.619745] 
[00003.619745]         The following aliases can be used to reference common sets of gate
[00003.619746]         types:
[00003.619746]           simple: AND OR XOR MUX
[00003.619747]           cmos2:  NAND NOR
[00003.619747]           cmos3:  NAND NOR AOI3 OAI3
[00003.619748]           cmos4:  NAND NOR AOI3 OAI3 AOI4 OAI4
[00003.619748]           cmos:   NAND NOR AOI3 OAI3 AOI4 OAI4 NMUX MUX XOR XNOR
[00003.619749]           gates:  AND NAND OR NOR XOR XNOR ANDNOT ORNOT
[00003.619749]           aig:    AND NAND OR NOR ANDNOT ORNOT
[00003.619750] 
[00003.619750]         The alias 'all' represent the full set of all gate types.
[00003.619751] 
[00003.619751]         Prefix a gate type with a '-' to remove it from the list. For example
[00003.619752]         the arguments 'AND,OR,XOR' and 'simple,-MUX' are equivalent.
[00003.619752] 
[00003.619752]         The default is 'all,-NMUX,-AOI3,-OAI3,-AOI4,-OAI4'.
[00003.619753] 
[00003.619753]     -dff
[00003.619754]         also pass $_DFF_?_ and $_DFFE_??_ cells through ABC. modules with many
[00003.619754]         clock domains are automatically partitioned in clock domains and each
[00003.619755]         domain is passed through ABC independently.
[00003.619755] 
[00003.619756]     -clk [!]<clock-signal-name>[,[!]<enable-signal-name>]
[00003.619756]         use only the specified clock domain. this is like -dff, but only FF
[00003.619757]         cells that belong to the specified clock domain are used.
[00003.619757] 
[00003.619757]     -keepff
[00003.619758]         set the "keep" attribute on flip-flop output wires. (and thus preserve
[00003.619758]         them, for example for equivalence checking.)
[00003.619759] 
[00003.619759]     -nocleanup
[00003.619760]         when this option is used, the temporary files created by this pass
[00003.619760]         are not removed. this is useful for debugging.
[00003.619761] 
[00003.619761]     -showtmp
[00003.619762]         print the temp dir name in log. usually this is suppressed so that the
[00003.619762]         command output is identical across runs.
[00003.619762] 
[00003.619763]     -markgroups
[00003.619763]         set a 'abcgroup' attribute on all objects created by ABC. The value of
[00003.619764]         this attribute is a unique integer for each ABC process started. This
[00003.619764]         is useful for debugging the partitioning of clock domains.
[00003.619765] 
[00003.619765]     -dress
[00003.619766]         run the 'dress' command after all other ABC commands. This aims to
[00003.619768]         preserve naming by an equivalence check between the original and
[00003.619769]         post-ABC netlists (experimental).
[00003.619769] 
[00003.619770]     -liberty_args <string>
[00003.619770]         when -liberty is used, also pass the specified arguments to ABC
[00003.619771]         command "read_lib". Example: -liberty_args "-G 250"
[00003.619771] 
[00003.619771] When no target cell library is specified the Yosys standard cell library is
[00003.619772] loaded into ABC before the ABC script is executed.
[00003.619772] 
[00003.619773] Note that this is a logic optimization pass within Yosys that is calling ABC
[00003.619773] internally. This is not going to "run ABC on your design". It will instead run
[00003.619774] ABC on logic snippets extracted from your design. You will not get any useful
[00003.619774] output when passing an ABC script that writes a file. Instead write your full
[00003.619775] design as BLIF file with write_blif and then load that into ABC externally if
[00003.619775] you want to use ABC to convert your design into another format.
[00003.619776] 
[00003.619776] [1] http://www.eecs.berkeley.edu/~alanmi/abc/
[00003.619776] 
